(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608319601-the-jvm-free-project-linker-was-one-printer")
  (:adr/title "JVM-free project linker \u306e\u6b63\u4f53\u306f printer \u4e00\u3064\u3060\u3063\u305f \u2014 \u691c\u8a3c\u5668 3 \u672c\u3092 nbb \u3078\u79fb\u3057\u3001amu \u306e source-text \u304c cljs \u306e BigInt \u3092\u8aad\u307f\u76f4\u305b\u306a\u3044\u5f62\u3067\u5370\u5b57\u3057\u3066\u3044\u305f\u306e\u3092\u76f4\u3059")
  (:adr/status :accepted)
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")
  (:adr/deciders "owner")
  (:adr/repos (vector "kotoba-lang/amu" "kotoba-lang/aiueos"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608319400-the-cid-becomes-the-identity-and-an-oracle-that-runs-the-object" "adr-2608136000-six-questions" "aiueos-ADR-0128"))
  (:adr/body "## Decision\n\nThe verifiers that execute Kotoba admission objects run on nbb with no JVM.\n`os/aiueos/scripts/verify-admissions.cljs` replaces three `.clj` programs; the\nJVM aggregate keeps the other ten and its receipt names where the three went.\n\n## What was actually in the way\n\nOwner instruction (2026-08-31): *clj \u3058\u3083\u306a\u304f\u3066 kotoba, amu \u3067*.\n\nThe three verifiers called `kotoba.compiler.core/compile-project`, which is\n`.clj` and has no portable sibling. Everything under it already was portable:\n`kotoba.compiler.project` (the linker), `kotoba.sema` (the frontend) and\n`kotoba.kir` (the lowering and the interpreter) are all `.cljc`. The JVM was\ncarried by one thin wrapper -- and by one printer.\n\n`link-source` emits the linked module graph as text and the caller reads it\nback. On ClojureScript the reader returns integer literals as JS `BigInt`,\n`pr-str` renders a BigInt as `#object[BigInt 42]`, and the reader answers\n`unsupported reader dispatch` on the way in. **The portable linker therefore\nfailed for every project containing a number.** That is the JVM-free project\nlinker gap the workspace Q9 rule names as a blocker, and it was a printer.\n\nMeasured against aiueos's 83 `.kotoba` objects: the seven that reached the\nemit step all died there, and the other 76 only looked healthier because they\nhad already been rejected for a real reason. With the printer fixed (amu#714)\nfour link, and the remaining three fail with the same reasons the JVM path\nalready reports for them -- the two paths agree.\n\n## The test that was already there\n\n`test/nbb/project.cljs` has run `link-source` on nbb since it was written, and\npassed. Its two fixture modules are strings only and contain no integer\nanywhere: the right function, on the right runtime, never touching the defect.\nIt now also links a pair of modules that do arithmetic and asserts the linked\ntext carries no `#object`. Reverting only `source-text` fails exactly that\nassertion and leaves the older one passing.\n\n## What this costs, measured rather than asserted\n\nAgainst amu 0085e138, identical vectors and identical verdicts to the JVM\nrunners: `cid-v1-admit` 14 vectors + 1 trap in 242 s, `unixfs-file-admit` 22\nvectors in 0.4 s, `value-runtime-cas-verify` 6 vectors in 179 s -- against\nroughly 197 s and 120 s for the two JVM runners that hashed the same 12 KiB\nblocks. The ClojureScript interpreter is somewhat slower at SHA-256 and that\nis the whole of the difference. The contract that hashes nothing is faster\nhere than it was on the JVM.\n\n`node --stack-size=4000` is required: `value-runtime-sha256` is 9 KB of nested\n`if` and the ClojureScript analyzer recurses deeply enough over it to exhaust\nnode's default stack. The runner says so rather than reporting a module it\ncould not analyse as absent.\n\n## Pins, and the one not taken\n\n`:verify-admissions` is a separate deps alias with its own amu pin. Advancing\n`:test` instead would have moved that pin 255 commits and re-measured ten\ncommitted receipts as a side effect of adding a runner. Its kotoba-kir is\npinned explicitly too: amu's own pin predates `kir/execute`'s memory image,\nand without it every vector traps `:kernel-memory-unavailable` -- which the\nrunner refuses to report as a pass, and is how that was caught.\n\n## Four refusals instead of a green line\n\nNo declared compiler pin (checked first, so a run without one does not spend\nsix minutes before refusing); a contract declaring no vectors; a kotoba-kir\nthat cannot execute kernel memory; an analyzer that exhausts the stack. Each\nprints REFUSING TO REPORT A PASS and names which.\n\n## Three defects found by reviewing this change rather than by running it\n\n- The JVM aggregate's case list lost two of the three moved objects but kept\n  `value-runtime-cas-verify`, whose `.clj` had been deleted. The receipt would\n  have shipped 11 objects and 8 failing: a fabricated red from `load-file` on a\n  missing script. It is 10 and 7, the pre-existing set.\n- The runner resolved its compiler pin while printing the receipt, so a run\n  with no pin executed every vector and refused afterwards. Correct verdict,\n  wrong order.\n- All three contracts still named the deleted `.clj` paths in\n  `:verification :runner`.\n\n## Not claimed\n\nThis changes which host runs the verifiers. It does not link any object into\n`KERNEL.ELF` -- that still needs the object-producing compiler advance recorded\nin aiueos ADR-0128, which regenerates all 37 committed objects and every pinned\ndigest in `build-uefi.sh`.\n\n**Correction, same day.** The first version of this section said nine of the\nremaining verifiers still model their object and could all be converted the way\n`cas-verify` was. Measured rather than assumed, the number was one. Of the ten\nleft in the JVM aggregate, seven cannot be converted at all because their\nobjects do not compile (upstream amu#625) -- writing an executing verifier for\nthem would be writing code that cannot run -- and two already executed. The\ntenth, `value-handle-arena`, could not be executed either, because its first\nact is to take the arena lock and `kir/execute` refused the lock pair outright.\nRemoving that refusal (kotoba-kir 674a5bee) is what made it one instead of\nnone; it is now the fourth contract in the nbb runner, and the aggregate holds\nnine.")))
